#
# Template for the Android TV Remote appliance's .env file. Copy to .env and edit; every
# value below is optional and the documented default is what the container uses when the
# variable is absent, so an empty .env yields a working, unauthenticated LAN appliance.
# The application-facing variables mirror the configuration table in
# docs/design/architecture.md exactly; the HOST_* and IMAGE_* variables are consumed by
# docker-compose rather than by the server.

# --- Host-side deployment knobs (read by docker-compose, not by the server) -------------

# Host directory bind-mounted onto the container's DATA_DIR. This is the NFS-backed path
# that makes pairing durable: it holds devices.db and certs/<device_id>/{cert,key}.pem.
# It must exist and be writable by the container, or the entrypoint refuses to start.
HOST_DATA_DIR=/volumes/androidtvremote

# Image name/tag built and run by the stack.
IMAGE_NAME=androidtv-remote
IMAGE_TAG=latest

# UID:GID the container runs as. It must be able to write HOST_DATA_DIR. 1000:1000 is the
# first user on a typical single-user Linux host. If HOST_DATA_DIR is an NFS export owned
# by a different uid, set these to match rather than loosening the export's permissions;
# a mismatch makes the entrypoint fail loudly at boot instead of losing pairings.
PUID=1000
PGID=1000

# --- Application configuration (see docs/design/architecture.md) ------------------------

# Container-side root for the SQLite registry and per-device certificates.
# Changing this requires changing the mount target in docker-compose.yml to match.
DATA_DIR=/volumes/androidtvremote

# TCP port the appliance listens on. With network_mode: host this is the host port too.
PORT=7433

# Address the HTTP server binds to.
HOST=0.0.0.0

# When set, every /api and /mcp request must carry `Authorization: Bearer <token>` or
# `?token=<token>`. LEAVING THIS COMMENTED OUT (the default) MEANS NO AUTHENTICATION AT
# ALL: anyone who can reach the port can control every paired TV. That is intentional for
# a trusted home LAN, where a fresh `docker compose up` must produce a working remote with
# zero configuration. Uncomment and set a long random value if the appliance is reachable
# from anywhere less trusted. /healthz and the SPA's static assets stay unauthenticated
# either way.
#   REMOTE_API_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
# REMOTE_API_TOKEN=

# Name displayed on the Android TV during the pairing handshake.
CLIENT_NAME=Android TV Remote

# Run the background mDNS browser. Set to false on Docker Desktop / bridge networking,
# where link-local multicast cannot reach the container and discovery would silently
# return nothing forever; add devices by IP instead.
DISCOVERY_ENABLED=true

# Seconds between full mDNS rescans.
DISCOVERY_INTERVAL=60

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PUBLIC_URL — the HTTPS origin this appliance is ALSO reachable at.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave this unset unless you have put the appliance behind HTTPS. It is purely advisory:
# the appliance never connects to it, and setting it changes nothing about how the server
# listens. It exists for one reason — installing the remote as a phone app.
#
# WHY IT IS NEEDED
#   Browsers only install a Progressive Web App, and only run a service worker, from a
#   "secure context". https:// and http://localhost qualify; http://192.168.1.50:7433 does
#   NOT, no matter how correct the manifest is. So on a bare LAN address the install button
#   cannot work, and the Settings screen hides the Install section entirely rather than
#   explaining a dead end you cannot act on.
#
#   Set PUBLIC_URL and that changes: someone opening the remote over plain HTTP gets a link
#   to your HTTPS origin, and installs it from there. Open the HTTPS origin directly and
#   the real install button appears.
#
# HOW TO GET ONE (any of these works — pick whatever you already run)
#   - A reverse proxy you already have (nginx, openresty, Caddy, Traefik) terminating TLS
#     on a hostname that resolves to the appliance, with a Let's Encrypt certificate.
#   - A Cloudflare Tunnel, which needs no port forwarding and no public inbound firewall
#     rule at all.
#   - Tailscale or a similar mesh VPN with HTTPS enabled on the tailnet name.
#
#   Whichever you use, the proxy must forward WebSocket upgrades to /api/ws, or the UI will
#   load and then sit there never receiving live state.
#
# SECURITY — READ THIS BEFORE PUTTING IT ON THE PUBLIC INTERNET
#   Giving the appliance a public hostname does NOT make it private. If the origin is
#   reachable from the internet and REMOTE_API_TOKEN is unset, anyone who resolves the name
#   can list your televisions and their MACs and internal IPs, power them off, launch apps,
#   and type into on-screen search boxes. Either keep the origin restricted (firewall rule,
#   VPN-only DNS, or Cloudflare Access in front) or set REMOTE_API_TOKEN above. Preferably
#   both.
#
# Must be https:// — the appliance refuses to start with an http:// value here, because
# advertising a plaintext origin would send people somewhere the install still cannot work.
#
# Example: PUBLIC_URL=https://remote.example.net
PUBLIC_URL=

# CSP frame-ancestors for the /embed view. Restrict this to your home dashboard's origin
# (e.g. https://home.example.lan) if the appliance is reachable beyond a trusted LAN.
FRAME_ANCESTORS=*

# Log level for uvicorn and the application logger: critical|error|warning|info|debug|trace
LOG_LEVEL=info

# Comma-separated allowed CORS origins for /api, or * for any.
CORS_ORIGINS=*

# --- Development only (docker-compose.dev.yml) ------------------------------------------

# Port the Vite dev server listens on; it proxies /api, /healthz and the WebSocket to the
# API container on PORT so the SPA runs same-origin in dev exactly as it does in prod.
WEB_DEV_PORT=7434
